## 3. Test Plan

### Execution and evidence ordering

1. Complete T-001 via the returned canonical preparation route after TP approval. Capture the new SD/TP hashes and exact baseline, including archived versus retained work; inspect worker ownership, assembler propagation and supported local host registration before editing. A dirty candidate implementation path requires explicit isolation/reconciliation rather than overwriting it.
2. T-002 to T-005 verify exact dependency resolution, resource build, default/cockpit inventory, owned assembly and scoped connection config. Reuse existing implementations only after the renewed baseline assessment. First implement/test the bounded independent read registry and MCP-only worker/capture limits plus the completion schema, retaining the already implemented optional initial Run path without a second snapshot capture, URL/storage persistence or eager context publication. Before T-006, minimally bind each reader/controller to its immutable view session and expose capacity feedback; full publication ownership and handoff UI remain T-008/T-010 after the checkpoint. Prepare schema and resource together and qualify a fresh loaded descriptor. Do not install into plugin cache or silently edit global host configuration. Produce the reviewable entry before any activation step that needs host permission. If activation cannot be performed, preserve prepared output and identify the host gap.
3. T-006 is the early feasibility checkpoint. Record host version/build, Node/platform, bridge protocol, advertised serverTools/display modes, actual selected mode, exact server/UI/lock digests, named loaded connection, method responses and rendered observations. Include real empty and direct named opening, exact requested versus displayed identity, missing/invalid feedback, actual available width and two views reading independently through the same loaded connection. Separate original protocol feasibility from current two-view reading and final production handoff; do not repeat a synthetic message automatically. Synthetic protocol probes are labelled; they establish feasibility only. The final source/packet journey remains T-012.
4. T-007 to T-010 implement the selected product path with deterministic fixture/fake-clock/deferred-response tests where required to reproduce boundaries and races. Then T-011 runs consolidated compatibility and routed reviews. Do not equate scenario rows with test counts or weaken assertions to fit the implementation.
5. T-012 records the complete actual-host journey, failures, no-write window, rollback/reconnect and setup/knowledge evidence. Canonical QA determines pass/revise/block; human QA/UAT approvals and applicable OR remain separate. The TP predicts no successful outcome.

### Automated checks and planned test ownership

Use supported Node 22.22.3 already available, preserving project engine support. Changed and additional behavioral coverage belongs beside existing owners, including packages/core/test/cockpit-session-test.js for registry/publication ownership and packages/control-ui/test/handoff.test.tsx for immutable ports and two-sided release: `packages/core/test/cockpit-context-test.js` for graph/packet/limits; `packages/cli/scripts/cockpit-mcp-test.js` for strict local mode/session/composition/provenance; existing `packages/mcp-server` tests for SDK registration/resource/tool wire behavior; existing private UI test lanes for shared transport, rendering, bridge controller and build. These are planned files/coverage, not commands already executed.

Run the resulting Core/CLI tests with Node; run `npm --prefix packages/mcp-server test`, `npm --prefix packages/control-ui run typecheck`, `npm --prefix packages/control-ui run build`, `npm --prefix packages/control-ui test` and `npm --prefix packages/control-ui run test:browser`. Reuse the existing npm --prefix packages/control-ui run build:mcp script under the Vite owner; record exact command/output and assembled resource digest. Protocol tests must cover supported negotiated protocol eras where SDK/host actually support them; do not claim unsupported-era compatibility from schema inspection.

Run affected existing regressions: `packages/core/test/control-state-test.js`, `packages/cli/scripts/control-command-test.js`, `packages/cli/scripts/control-inspect-test.js`, `packages/core/test/interaction-presentation-test.js`, `packages/cli/scripts/plugin-mcp-runtime-test.js`, `packages/cli/scripts/mcp-lifecycle-test.js`, `packages/cli/scripts/runtime-integrity-layout-test.js`, `packages/cli/scripts/runtime-integrity-negative-test.js`, `packages/cli/scripts/local-preparation-test.js`, `packages/cli/scripts/package-contents-test.js` and `packages/cli/scripts/payload-budget-test.js`. Also run assembler/local-development tests that own changed profile/preparation branches. Existing verification owners continue supplying required checks; no new parallel verification framework. Public/default inventory comparison is meaningful because UI dependencies must stay private. If assembly needs generated prerequisites, use the existing synchronization/preparation owner before testing; record exact output profile and prerequisite ordering.

Boundary tests use disposable controlled files/registered sources, not huge live repository captures. Fake clocks cover expiry without hours of waiting. Inject controlled source mutation/read/bridge responses for race reproduction, retaining at least one real stdio integration and real captured filesystem lane. Capture response/packet UTF-8 sizes using the same serialized form sent to the host. Source/schema/default-profile failure tests must prove the actual production boundary, not a test-only guard.

### Manual and actual-host checks

Check card, expanded detail and passive source at 320/560/800/1280px in the browser, measuring container width, scrollWidth, columns and header height rather than assuming the host viewport. In the native host record the actual available container/display mode separately; do not infer native width from browser screenshots. Exercise the 640px container breakpoint, search without selection changes, counted expandable source notices, unchanged Pages branding and readable wrapping. Use keyboard and pointer journeys at supported narrow/wide sizes and the actual native panel/display mode: compact entry, explicit run selection, source identity, passive artefact, maintained graph node, inclusion/exclusion, preparation, context acceptance and deliberate question. Custom follow-up uses Codex's existing composer. Verify visible context/source identity without claiming acknowledgement proves a correct answer. Preserve source/protocol, browser, actual-host and human-UAT results separately; fixture/browser successes cannot satisfy a missing actual-host criterion.

The no-write window hashes canonical control bytes immediately before and after app-only operations, including context and question. No agent control bookkeeping or graph curation occurs inside that window. Later evidence writes occur outside it and cannot be used to conceal an app mutation. A live later delivery request, if exercised, uses the installed independent connection and fresh canonical binding; never send an approval string or implementation request as the app's test question.

Evidence records scenario ID, exact fixture or project source identity, command/action, observed expected/actual outcome, time, runtime/UI digest, and any failure/uncertainty. Reports use bounded logs without session secrets. Test skips, inaccessible capabilities and stale earlier results remain gaps. Cooperative self-review is labelled and never described as independent review.

### Per-view implementation and verification boundaries

The Core session service remains the sole owner of the bounded connection registry and exclusive publication record. Extend the existing pool/worker/reader constructor options for MCP-specific limits; the browser default remains unchanged. Use the production strict contract for the new completion operation and the existing typed DTO failure lane. No parallel scheduler or approval authority is created.

T-003/T-004 prove retirement counting before worker replacement and connection shutdown; queued/active cancellation cannot release capacity before actual teardown. Each view has its own opaque IDs, immutable capture, timers and read generation. T-008 proves reserve/prepare/validate/begin/acknowledged-complete/uncertain transitions at that existing owner. T-009/T-010 prove immutable session ports, nonowner no-publication, acknowledgement ordering and completion-only retry through actual production controllers. Cooperative completion does not prove human authority or independent host context erasure.

Replace obsolete tests asserting that a different render expires an earlier session. Retain scoped expiry, strict selector, stale own-selection and own-bootstrap tests with their intended invariants. A legitimate change to that obsolete assertion is not permission to skip tests or soften unrelated boundaries. Use existing Node test/fake-pool/fake-clock seams for controlled allocation and teardown; supplement them with real worker capture and stdio schema lanes. Large byte boundary fixtures are disposable, bounded and run in the smallest meaningful number of configurations.

The final native two-view window includes equal-Run and distinct-Run reading, own source/close behavior, refused competing handoff, acknowledged release and subsequent deliberate transfer. The native host must supply genuinely concurrent reachable views on the same connection; sequential screenshots alone do not establish independence. If automation exposes only one old/expired panel, retain prepared runtime and record this as an actual-host gap, without silently asking the user to reapprove completed gates. Fresh connection recovery must separately qualify host-context currentness; an empty server record is not proof that historical context was cleared.

After verified implementation, update docs/architecture/06-agdf-cockpit.md and affected existing MCP/architecture cross-references to reflect implemented registry, limits, wire release flow, responsibilities and recovery. Until then the approved SD describes the new design and architecture documentation describes the existing implementation. Curate only verified outcomes through the existing closeout knowledge owner.

## 4. Brownfield Scope

Inspect and reuse `packages/core/lib/control-read/`, `control-inspect/cockpit.js`, run parser/evaluator and Core composition; `packages/control-ui/server/pool.mjs`, `read-worker.mjs`, HTTP/session wrappers, `src/App.tsx`, `state.ts`, `api.ts`, `types.ts`, existing views and Vite owner; `packages/mcp-server/bin/`, `src/` and its tests; `packages/cli/lib/mcp-dispatch-runtime.js`, local development preparation and `mcp-lifecycle/package.js`; `scripts/assemble-npm.mjs` and owned profile/package checks. The approved SD owns module boundaries and versions. Wrapper compatibility is verified rather than retaining a second worker implementation.

Only current-run control artefacts and necessary implementation/derived paths belong to this scope. Existing dirty MASTER_BACKLOG/current-run records are process state, not permission to alter unrelated runs. `.agdf/control/CONTEXT_GRAPH.md` remains maintained knowledge; curate verified implementation facts into existing appropriate nodes through closeout, preserving the open impact until evidence exists. Do not create graph relationships merely from planning. Installed AGDF/default runtime, protected host settings, unrelated projects and the existing browser process are not general repair targets.

## 5. Out Of Scope

Graph editor/database, inferred relevance, recursive context crawling, automatic completeness/Soll-Ist evaluation, generic filesystem/shell/network tools, gate/approval writers, automatic contextual questions, a second chat composer, persistent app selection/packet storage, remote/multiuser/public deployment, broad plugin/cache repair, new host adapters, public SDK/runtime migration, commit/push/PR/release/publication and transfer of prior browser-run approvals. No architecture/version fallback without the applicable source revision route. This change adds no server per card, persistent session registry, inter-iframe bus or host adapter.

## 6. Risks And Blockers

| Risk or trigger | Required response and evidence owner |
|---|---|
| Exact SDK packages unavailable, incompatible or contaminate the owned server graph | Stop dependency/build lane; record registry/build evidence and route SD revision before substitution; UI/MCP owner |
| Actual host cannot load named app, provide usable larger view or accept required context/message methods | T-006 blocks extensive dependent feature work; preserve precise feasibility gap and fallback, route design/evidence owner; never declare embedded completion from mocks |
| Protected configuration/registration workflow unavailable | Preserve ready reviewable entry and use supported authorized workflow only; external permission/state gap stays explicit, not a silently edited global config |
| Source/selector/provenance mismatch, mutation, limit or invalidation uncertainty | Reject/block affected handoff with exact reason; Core/UI owner fixes and reruns affected scenarios |
| Browser/default MCP or owned assembly regression | Block QA, repair within approved scope and rerun directly affected compatibility checks; scope growth routes back to earliest affected gate |
| Stale descriptor/bootstrap, foreign or unavailable initial Run | Reject invalid selectors, retain requested identity and fresh-session generation checks; prepare matched schema/UI and record a fresh actual host discovery before claiming direct opening; MCP/UI owner |
| Publication ownership cannot be safely completed or host context outcome is uncertain | Keep exclusive handoff blocked while reads remain usable; require matched acknowledged completion or qualified fresh connection, never timed takeover; Core/UI and host evidence owners |
| Native host cannot expose two concurrent views on one connection | Keep SCN-059/060 and affected acceptance evidence open; record observed host limitation without treating sequential panels or browser fixtures as proof; host evidence owner |
| Missing live-host/no-write/rollback/graph knowledge evidence | Keep criterion/closeout open; QA/OR owners decide readiness, no presumed acceptance |
| Historical model context persists after lost host/process or later source edit | Record bounded observation and limitation; disable uncertain new handoff and require fresh validation for later use, without claiming historical messages can be erased |

No debt waiver, authority shortcut or second source of truth is accepted. A material implementation discovery that changes approved scope/design goes through canonical revision rather than editing approved PRD/SD bytes.

## 7. Next Step

Review and approve only with `Approval: TP`. Valid approval permits the routed Brownfield Analysis and then scoped implementation once its prerequisites are satisfied. It does not approve QA/UAT/OR, protected host writes outside permission, version substitution or VCS/release actions.

## Source and Derivation Evidence

Approved SD source: SD.md, SHA-256 2ad7312e1e7cb5c94d84932fd5ee6600efc1732ca684d55e627fd65fd8780d54. Approved acceptance source: PRD.md, SHA-256 8f372986651c47aa376accb9044bdc2ad822db101270f1c76ac0ab08e41dcb1c. Preparation revision: d83596aa-b722-45de-a427-cd896b0ae0e5 (64), following deliberately recorded new Approval: SD. Source revision operation d42626e2-69b1-4cd7-9a9b-753b72d01651 archived prior SD/TP approval/presentation/binding evidence and reset preparation/execution/review fulfilment. This plan preserves twelve stable tasks, ten criteria and seven design decision IDs. It covers twenty criterion/design pairs, including AC-006/SDD-006, with sixty stable scenario IDs. SCN-047 through SCN-060 add independent-view, capacity, publication ownership and native evidence; obsolete render-supersession expectations are replaced without redefining PRD acceptance.

Current implementation and prior WORK_STEP_HIERARCHY_VERIFICATION-02.json, browser/protocol/native records are retained dated evidence, not renewed task fulfilment or proof of multi-view behavior. Optional Run opening and existing Pages layouts already exist and need reassessment, not a claim that they remain unimplemented. Independent sessions and the two-sided publication completion are not implemented; no implementation, build, activation or VCS action occurred during this TP drafting. T-001 establishes current source/dirty-path/runtime baseline before any code change. The exact approved SD/PRD and other approved artefacts are preserved byte-for-byte.

The semantic review checks this exact plan against approved source mappings, scope, limits, retained code and implementation/evidence order. Its machine proof is a cooperative derivation attestation, not an independent human review or an executed test. Future evidence records must add current commands, identities, outcomes and failures rather than treating a planning filename as proof.

## AGDF Approval Summary (de; source=en)

- Ziel und Umfang: Ein Server pro Verbindung mit höchstens vier unabhängigen aktiven oder noch aufzuräumenden Lesesitzungen. Eine neue Karte beendet keine andere; die fünfte Öffnung wird sichtbar abgewiesen. Zwölf bestehende Aufgaben, alle zehn PRD-Kriterien und sieben Designentscheidungen bleiben erhalten. Der Plan umfasst 20 Kriterien-/Design-Zuordnungen und 60 konkrete Szenarien; Produktakzeptanz und freigegebene Quellen bleiben unverändert.
- Vorbereitung und Umsetzung: Nach Approval: TP erfasst die erneute Brownfield Analysis den aktuellen Code, die behaltenen Änderungen, Quellhashes und betroffenen Owner. Dann folgen Core-Workergrenzen und Sitzungsregistry mit atomarer Kapazität, eigenen Timern und Selektoren, begrenzten Aufträgen, bestätigtem Workerabbau und Verbindungsschluss. Run-ID, gemeinsamer React-Leseweg, Browser und regulärer MCP-Modus werden über ihre vorhandenen Owner erhalten und frisch geprüft. Schema, Ressource und lokale Runtime werden zusammen gebaut; die neue Operation complete_context_invalidation wird streng validiert.
- Grenzen und Tests: Vier Ansichten mit je höchstens 64 MiB erfassten Quelldaten und 256 MiB Worker-Old-Generation, zusammen höchstens 256 MiB erfasste Quellen und acht aktive/wartende Worker-Aufträge. Diese Grenzen garantieren keinen gesamten Prozessspeicher. Inaktivität 30 Minuten und Gesamtdauer acht Stunden gelten je Ansicht; Browser-Grenzen bleiben unverändert. Geprüft werden gleichzeitige Öffnungen, Kapazität ohne Verdrängen, eigene Freigabe erst nach Workerabbau, Schließen/Ablauf/Fehler einer Ansicht, Fremdselektoren auch bei gleichem Run, verspätete Antworten, Shutdown und Grenzen ohne Abschneiden.
- Kontextübergabe: Core hält einen exklusiven temporären Eigentümer je Verbindung. Vorbereitung reserviert vor asynchroner Arbeit; konkurrierende Ansichten erhalten Belegt-Feedback und können weiter lesen. Invalidierung beginnt beim Eigentümer; erst bestätigte Host-Invalidierung und passender Abschluss geben die Übergabe frei. Nicht-Eigentümer und fehlgeschlagener Beginn senden keine Host-Invalidierung. Bei verlorener Abschlussantwort wird nur der Serverabschluss wiederholt. Veraltete/fremde IDs, spätes Aufräumen und ein neuer Bootstrap dürfen keine andere Ansicht übernehmen oder deren Kontext löschen. Unklare Bestätigung oder Eigentümerverlust blockiert weitere Übergaben, nicht das Lesen; kein zeitgesteuertes Übernehmen und keine automatische Fragewiederholung.
- Prüfungsfolge und fachliche UX: Früh wird im echten Codex die aktuelle Runtime samt Deskriptor, Öffnung mit/ohne Run-ID und gleichzeitiges Lesen zweier Ansichten geprüft; gekennzeichnete Kontext-/Frage-Proben bleiben getrennte bewusste Handlungen. Erst nach diesem Machbarkeitsnachweis folgen vollständige Übergabe, Regressionen und echte Zwei-Ansichten-Abnahme mit verweigerter Konkurrenz, bestätigter Freigabe und anschließender Übergabe der zweiten Ansicht. Pages-Branding, hell/dunkel, Containergrößen 320/560/800/1280, Navigation, Fokus und die fachliche Einheit aus Arbeitsschritt, Voraussetzungen und Nachweisen werden frisch geprüft. Die abgegrenzte App-Nutzung muss Kontrolldateien unverändert lassen.
- Nachweise und Entscheidung: Alte Testergebnisse bleiben historische Nachweise; die neue Umsetzung ist noch offen. Quell-/Build-, Protokoll-, Browser- und native Ergebnisse bleiben getrennt. Fehlende native Parallelansicht oder unklarer Host-Kontext verhindern vollständige Abnahme. Nach verifizierter Umsetzung folgen Architektur-/Einrichtungsdokumentation, Rücknahme und geprüfte Wissenspflege. Approval: TP erlaubt notwendige Vorbereitung und danach gebundene Umsetzung; CR, QA, UAT und regulärer Abschluss folgen separat. Keine neuen Freigabeschreiber, Hostadapter, Datenbank, Veröffentlichung, Release- oder Git-Aktion.
